hello I'm Susan - Stein and I am at Penn  State University Park in the Department  of Biochemistry and molecular biology  and in this first slide you can see me  standing next to a giant cryo electron  microscope so this is the Titan crius  and we use this microscope to collect  data to solve 3d reconstructions  so the project I'm going to talk about  today is using Crimea as a structural  analysis to understand how canine  parvovirus or CPB uses the transferrin  receptor and how this contributed  contributes to species jumping  so here on this slide I'm showing the  people who did the work on the Left  panel from left to right we have Surya  Dan and Hume walk Hume look is the one  who did all the reconstructions in my  lab on the right panel from left to  right we have Robert Ian and Colin  collin parish's lab is at cornell and  they collaborated with us on this  project  so prior to the late 1970s there was an  aversion of parvo virus that infected  dogs  the first parvo viruses are canine  parvovirus is that arose in the late 70s  started causing pandemics and which  means they swept across the world  while we when we started studying canine  parvovirus in collaboration with collin  parish's lab we found that there were  mutations between the feline version and  the canine version and originally we  thought that the virus must have jumped  species from cats to dogs since then  we've done more work and found that it's  a little more complicated than that  and it seems probably likely that the  virus jumped species from cats to  raccoons and then to dogs we were able  to map on the capsid surface the areas  the mutations where the mutations  cluster and we're able to deduce that  that is where the receptor binds  so as the through history as the virus  has circulated we've found that it  infects quite a number of carnivores and  you see the the tree on the left that  shows the relationship of these  different variants of the virus and on  the right we show a number of canines  and the virus can infect the TFR  transferrin receptors of all these  different species and doing some binding  assays we found that the transferrin  receptor for the black pack jackal and  that's the canine in the center of that  group binds the the capsid with pretty  high affinity so we picked that for  these structural studies going into the  studies we have a lot of information  about canine parvovirus capsids there  are multiple crystal structures and  high-resolution cryo-em structures from  which we can base this work  and there's also some information on the  transferrin receptor itself the Martin  Lawrence solved the crystal structure of  human TF are shown on the left and it's  made up of three basic domains up at the  top it has a well overall it has this  sort of butterfly shape so the tips of  the butterfly wings that's the apical  domain and we know that's the portion  that binds to the virus capsid then  there's sort of a sensor domain where  you see the two wings wrapped together  to make this butterfly shape and that's  a very stable structure so this is a  dimer but it's never found as a monomer  and solution we we typically see it as a  very stable dimeric complex on the right  is a structure from yifan chain that  shows TFR binding it's it's natural  ligand which is transparent so the  transparent are arranged around the  outside of the TFR molecule so that they  will bind at the wing region and I'll  show you more about this a little bit  later so this diagram shows different  species of TF are lined up together  and the things to note here is they are  very similar blue highlights we show a  couple of glycosylation sites for the  canine version of TFR and then we know  this red site the site outlined in  orange or red there is a very critical  one for virus binding  this project was first started back in  2007 and this very low resolution  structure shown on this slide that  previous work we have a fairly low  resolution structure where we show one  PFR in the red green and yellow one TFR  bound to the virus capsid it's sort of  blobby but I think that more important  things that we learn from this structure  was because the TFR molecule is so large  relative to the size of the capsid that  only a few receptors can bind it  probably can't get more than 20  receptors on a capsid at any one time  because there just will crash into each  other and prevent binding next to a  next-door neighbor we also found that  when we worked with the complexes if we  use more than about ten TFR per capsid  that ratio led to aggregation so we knew  moving forward that we'd have to focus  on solving this structure and in a  symmetric way so using our black-backed  jackal purified TFR and purified canine  capsids we made an incubation of about  40 FRS per each capsid then we vitrified  that solution and that robot for  vegetation is shown in the center and  then collected data on using the titan  kriyas  this is an image that shows a micrograph  this is the output from the Titan koreas  so this is that the complex shown as a  2d projection in a raw micro-cap  micrograph and the little boxed areas  are blown up on the right and you can  see the outline of the capsid and then  make out some TFRs bound to it so we had  about almost 4,000 micrographs and we  were able to collect one hundred and  fifty four thousand particles or so from  this data set and we use those for the  3d reconstruction even though there's  only there about forty FRS per capsid  but the first thing we did was average  all over the capsid to get as high  resolution as we possibly could and when  we do that those few TFRs that are bound  sort of just averaged out we don't see  them but we get a really nice  reconstruction of the capsid alone and  that's what's shown here to the right is  a single structural protein and we can  look at that in comparison with the  crystal structures and see that there's  no massive or global conformational  change induced by the binding of the  receptor but we did see some other  change and that's shown here so on the  left is canine parvovirus or CPV alone  it's never been incubated with receptor  and on the right is the map that I just  showed you but these two maps are  rendered differently so the color code  color code corresponds to flexibility  anything that's blue and grading toward  white and red so the blue is is very  stable and white is much more flexible  and you can see that the capsid alone is  very rigid and stable but after the  transferrin receptor has bound the  capsid parts become more flexible or  more dynamic and that's shown on the  right  you  so now if we go back to the the  icosahedral map shown here or the  averaged map  we want to probe this map and find where  the TFRs are bound so in order to do  that we're going to do a focused  refinement or first we're going to start  with a simbra approach and I'm going to  describe that in the next slide so the  there's six little maps shown here and  the one that's called Enosh or it for  initial is the map we used as a model to  start with so basically we took a low  resolution model of the transferrin  receptor and just stuck it on to the  capsid and we use this as a search model  and that icosahedron a a virgin map and  so we were searching the map trying to  line up and find the locations of the  various one to four bound transferrin  receptors basically we're rotating the  map looking for where TFRs are bound  relative to the capsid and the outputs  are shown in the first third fifth  seventh and ninth iterations of this  process and this is called a symmetry  mismatch approach and you can see by the  ninth iteration that we've sort of  aligned all the bound TFRs into the same  orientation and sort of piled that  density on top of itself to show a  single asymmetric map of the virus with  TFR bound  so that showed us or confirmed for us  that the binding event did take place at  the tip or that apical domain of the TFR  and at the tip of the threefold spike on  the virus we have a very nice high  resolution capsid structure it's near  atomic resolution the receptor density  is is better than our first blob ology  that I showed you before from 2007 but  we are hoping to improve it the first  thing we noticed was there's too much  volume for just TFR now on the next  slide I show you what I mean so now if  show you two views the capsid is down  below and the TFR density is more in the  center and on the left you see the  butterfly turns 90 degrees and see this  extra density around it when in the  right view you can see the butterfly  sort of weighing on and you see that  cluster of density unfilled density  above well we know from Yvonne Chang  structure that this is this unfilled  density is where TF transferrin binds to  the transferrin receptor so our  transferrin receptor had TF already  associated with it when it bound to the  virus we went back to the purified TF r  alone and we did charge detection mass  spectrometry and this showed that an  analysis of purified TF r showed that we  had free TF r with one TF bound and  somewhat two TF TF molecules bound and  the peak at the left might be where TF r  has dissociated which is sort of  unexpected to us in this complementary  experiment  moving forward  now we want to look at the interaction  between the receptor and the virus  capsid and when we zoom into this the  virus ISM is below and the TFR is above  in green we can see that at this site of  the interaction the density isn't very  good and so now to the right we see a  map that's rendered according to  resolution red is good high resolution  and you can see the capsid is mostly red  but as you start looking at the TFR  that's bound there the resolution  quickly becomes quite poor but now true  there's only a few TFRs compared to the  number of capsules we have but this  isn't anywhere near as good as what we  would expect we would expect it to be a  little better so we continued the  analysis so the first thing we did with  this analysis was the focused  classification so that means we masked  an area and we masked off an area  corresponding to the TFR volume and what  we found with this first round of focus  classification was occupied and  unoccupied TFR binding sites  then with the we did a second round a  focus classification and here we found  that there are multiple ways that TFR  binds relative to the capsid so in that  volume the TFR is actually taking on  different orientations relative to the  capsid so there's different positions of  the TFR adjacent to the capsid so if we  link all of these different orientations  together we actually see that there's  movement so the TFR binds the capsid  with a very dynamic movement it actually  rocks in three different directions and  there's a slight rotation so in the lab  we started calling this the  rock-and-roll receptor  the next thing we wanted to do was get  the very best resolution reconstruction  of TFR that we could so we released it  from the context of the capsid and just  did a reconstruction on TFR alone we got  to about 6 angstrom resolution which is  good enough to do the fitting so what we  did here was fit the crystal structure  salt solved by martin lawrence into the  cryo-em density and now we can put it  back into the context with the capsid  and interpret that interphase and that's  shown on the next slide so this is what  we call a road map and you can just  barely see the black triangle that  outlines one repeating subunit on the  surface of the virus the outlines show  the receptor binding site of TFR on the  capsid so we've changed the terminology  since I made this slide because outlined  in red are the sites that are consistent  between every binding mode as the TFR  sort of rocks and rolls on the capsid so  we always have those interactions the  ones outlined in yellow change a little  bit as the virus receptor rocks and  rolls then the two pictures on the right  show where the TFR is binding the capsid  so that's a TFR rotated and unrotated to  show the interactions of the different  amino acids on it on the transferrin  receptor  so the take-home message here is that we  believe that the variability with which  the capsid can interact with TFR is  really a driving force for the evolution  and explains how this virus can jump  species and use different species TFR  and still infect a cat a dog or  black-backed jackal  the last slide is another picture  showing acknowledgments for my lab  collin parish's lab and of course our  funding agencies this ends my talk but I  understand in this format there will be  questions and I'll be glad to follow up  with those questions by email
 

